# ============================================================================
# Homelab custom Suricata rules — C2 / adversary emulation (Sliver).
# Detection-as-code companion to the Wazuh local_rules.xml. Loaded via the
# `rule-files:` list in suricata.yaml; sids in the 9100000+ lab-local range.
#
# Context: a Sliver HTTPS *beacon* on a CORP host (fs-01, 10.10.10.20) calling
# home to the attacker box atk-01 (REDTEAM, 10.10.40.119:443). The C2 is TLS 1.3,
# so the certificate is encrypted in the handshake (Suricata logs tls.subject =
# None) and ET Open's cert/content signatures never fire. These two rules close
# that gap from different angles — a server-side TLS fingerprint and the
# beaconing behavior itself. Full writeup: phase-5-offense/sliver-c2/.
# ============================================================================

# (1) Sliver / Go C2 over HTTPS — implant TLS fingerprint (JA3, client-side).
# The Sliver beacon's Go crypto/tls ClientHello has a stable JA3
# (725543c78edf669194c11dc7a039b56e). Deliberately matched on the CLIENT ja3,
# not the server ja3s: JA3S lives in the ServerHello (to_client), and a
# `-> dest 443` rule is evaluated to_server, so a ja3s.hash rule here silently
# never fires — the ClientHello ja3 is on the to_server side and matches. (The
# server ja3s is f4febc55ea12b31ae17cfb7e614afda8 if a to_client rule is ever
# wanted.) Scoped to REDTEAM:443 — a CORP host speaking Go-TLS to the attacker
# segment is the anomaly. NB: Go-TLS JA3 is shared by other Go clients, so it is
# paired with the destination scope and the beaconing rule (2), not trusted alone.
alert tls $HOME_NET any -> 10.10.40.0/24 443 (msg:"LAB SLIVER C2 HTTPS beacon - Go-TLS JA3 implant fp to REDTEAM (T1071.001)"; flow:established,to_server; ja3.hash; content:"725543c78edf669194c11dc7a039b56e"; classtype:trojan-activity; sid:9100001; rev:2; metadata:mitre_tactic TA0011, mitre_technique T1071_001;)

# (2) Beaconing behavior — repeated short-lived connections from one internal
# host to a REDTEAM host on 443. Encryption-agnostic: it catches the *pattern*
# (regular automated check-ins) even if the TLS fingerprint rotates. At the
# beacon's ~5s interval a source produces >10 SYNs/min, so 10-in-60s fires while
# ordinary browsing (which reuses connections) does not.
alert tcp $HOME_NET any -> 10.10.40.0/24 443 (msg:"LAB C2 beaconing - repeated conns to REDTEAM:443 (T1071)"; flags:S; detection_filter:track by_src, count 10, seconds 60; classtype:trojan-activity; sid:9100002; rev:1; metadata:mitre_tactic TA0011, mitre_technique T1071;)
